The systems tell the frontend when the game reads a controller and where the Saturn's RAM is.

Two things Chimera needs that Mednafen's own program does not. A frame in
which the game did not take the controllers' state is a lag frame, so each
system calls MDFN_ChimeraInputRead where its game code is handed a pad's
state: the Saturn's port registers and its peripheral-data command, the
Virtual Boy's and the PC-FX's pad data registers, the Lynx's joystick and
switch registers. And the Saturn's two work RAMs are private to ss.cpp and
kept as 16-bit words, so it tells where they are with
MDFN_ChimeraWordMemory, for RAM Watch and RAM Search. The other three
systems already tell their RAM through MDFNMP_AddRAM.

--- a/src/mednafen.h
+++ b/src/mednafen.h
@@ -26,6 +26,17 @@
 
 MDFN_HIDE extern NativeVFS NVFS;
 
+#if defined(CHIMERA_CORE)
+// chimera: what the systems tell the frontend.
+// The game has just been given the state of a controller. A frame in which
+// this is not called is a lag frame: the game did not look at its input.
+void MDFN_ChimeraInputRead(void);
+// A memory of the machine that is kept as 16-bit words in the host's byte
+// order, with the address the machine's processor finds it at. (Memories kept
+// as bytes are told through MDFNMP_AddRAM, as they always were.)
+void MDFN_ChimeraWordMemory(const char* name, uint32 address, uint16* words, uint32 bytes);
+#endif
+
 // Points to a dynamically-allocated object, created with data from Emulated*.
 MDFN_HIDE extern MDFNGI* MDFNGameInfo;
 
